          %h3#InGeometricHomotopyTheory In geometric homotopy theory
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+topos+theory"} higher topos theory
            there is an elegant and powerful definition and construction of associated bundles. We discuss here some basics and how this recovers the traditional definition. For more see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+infinity-bundle"} associated infinity-bundle
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+representations+and+associated+bundles"} geometry of physics – representations and associated bundles
          %p
            At
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+principal+bundles"} geometry of physics – principal bundles
            in the section
            %em
              %a{:href => "geometry%20of%20physics%20--%20principal%20bundles#PrincipalBundlesViaSmoothGroupoids"} Smooth principal bundles via smooth groupoids
            is discussed how smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundles"} principal bundles
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
